From b87a97b0dfff5520cd6d2ddde7d8fad7c54513d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivel=C3=A4?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 09:31:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 23/31] favicons: draw an icon again when the colour scheme
 changes

An SVG favicon can follow prefers-color-scheme, as chatgpt.com's does:
black under a light scheme, white under a dark one. The engine draws it
under the scheme the page is told, keeps the drawing in the profile, and
before this kept showing it after the application's scheme changed.

Three things stood in the way.

The web theme took the new scheme, and nothing told the pages: the
WebContents only reread it on NotifyOnNativeThemeUpdated, which nothing
called. The one QStyleHints connection, made once rather than once per
adapter, now calls it when the scheme changes, and only then, so a page
on show is told the new scheme.

The favicon driver and the profile's favicon service now observe the web
theme, and act on every notice rather than keeping a scheme of their
own: one built before the engine first reads the application's scheme,
as the first page and profile of a run are, would otherwise start from
Chromium's default and miss the change back to it. The driver marks its
page's icon out of date, as a reload does, and fetches it again, at once
for a loaded page and from the candidates a page still loading reports
once it has. The service marks every drawing the profile keeps out of
date, so a page not on show draws its icon afresh on its next visit,
after showing the earlier drawing as an expired icon is shown.

A drawing at the address the icon already had was dropped: the driver
updated the navigation entry, and told the view, only when the address
changed. It now also does when the drawing differs in its pixels, so the
same drawing fetched again, as on a same-document navigation, is still
not reported. It reports such an icon as cleared and then as the
address again, the address on the next turn of the event loop, because
the Quick view reads its icon property a turn after it is told.

A platform that takes no colour scheme request, as the offscreen one
the tests run on does not, is told the change by the tests themselves:
they emit QStyleHints::colorSchemeChanged as a platform would.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
